<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferentino</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ferentinum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferentino, ancient Ferentinum, is a hill town of the Hernici in the Sacco valley of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It is known for its massive polygonal walls and its acropolis of the pre-Roman and Roman periods. It stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the Hernican revolt of 306 BCE and kept its independence as an ally before becoming a Roman municipium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferentino.jpg" alt="Ferentino" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fiat 500e · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiesa_di_Santa_Maria_Maggiore_(Ferentino).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
